## The headline problem: the two critical fixes from last time only *look* fixed

Both the tendency-tracking and chord-quality gaps got real engineering effort thrown at them this round — but neither actually works, because of the same underlying mistake in both cases: the string keys being matched don't correspond to the actual data.

**Tendency tracking.** `main.lua` now has a 10-case lookup mapping `activeItem.name` to a canonical tendency ID:

```lua
if name == "t-ti-d" then tendKey = "t-d"
elseif name == "t-fa-m" then tendKey = "f-m"
...
```

But check what `activeItem.name` is actually ever set to. For units-mode levels (1.x, 3.x, 5.x — where this data would come from), it's the unit's own `name` field straight out of `progression.lua`: `"t-d"`, `"f-m"`, `"r-d"`, `"fi-s"`, `"le-s"`, `"ra-d"`, `"te-d"`, `"me-r-d"`. None of those match the `"t-ti-d"` / `"t-fa-m"` / `"t-re-d"` style strings this new code is checking against — those strings don't exist anywhere in the codebase. Every comparison fails, `tendKey` is always `nil`, and the tendency system is exactly as dead as it was last review, just now behind 20 lines that look like a fix.

It's also structurally incomplete even setting that aside: for rule-generated melodies (`rule = {tendencies = N}`, e.g. levels 7.1, 11.1–11.2, 16.1–16.2), `activeItem.name` is whatever `engine.lua`'s procedural generator builds — a string like `"proc-11-12-2-0-"`. The tendency pair's actual ID (`"t-ti"`, `"t-fa"`, etc.) is used internally during block generation for the repetition guard, then discarded — it never makes it onto the returned object at all. So even with correct string matching, procedural-context tracking is impossible without a small change to `engine.lua` to carry that ID through. And there's still no code anywhere that checks incidental adjacent-pairs in pure-random melodies against the tendency tables — the third context is simply unbuilt.

**Chord quality.** Same shape of bug. `main.lua`:

```lua
local chordQual = (activeItem and activeItem.isStack) and (activeItem.chordQuality or activeItem.name) or nil
```

`activeItem.chordQuality` never exists — nothing sets it anywhere — so this always falls through to `activeItem.name`, which for stack levels is a Roman-numeral/voicing label: `"i"`, `"v7"`, `"i-6"`, `"vii-o7"`, `"iii+"`. `stats.lua`'s `chordQualities` table (now correctly including `half_diminished_7th` and `diminished_7th`, which was the gap I flagged) is keyed by `major_triad`, `dominant_7th`, etc. `prof.chordQualities["i"]` doesn't exist, the lookup fails silently, nothing gets recorded. Adding the missing keys was the right instinct, but it didn't matter — the actual quality string being passed in never matched any key, old or new.

The fix for both is the same shape of change: either build a translation table from unit `name` → canonical category (e.g. `"i"`→`major_triad`, `"v7"`→`dominant_7th`, `"vii-o7"`→`half_diminished_7th`) somewhere central, or — better — add the canonical key directly onto the unit definitions in `progression.lua` itself (a `quality = "major_triad"` field alongside `name`) so there's one source of truth instead of a string-guessing layer trying to reverse-engineer it from a label meant for something else.

